From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
Date: Fri, 12 Sep 2026 01:15:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 93/93] local: drm/msm/a6xx: reprogram the RSC sequencer after
 hibernate

FIX CANDIDATE for the GPU dying after a cross-boot hibernate resume, found by
measurement rather than inference, using the temporary 0092 instrumentation
(now shelved to docs.local/shelved-patches/ -- restore it if this ever needs
re-deriving).

a6xx_gmu_rpmh_init() programs the RSC/PDC sequencer exactly once, from
a6xx_gmu_init() at probe. After a cross-boot restore the block has been reset
and nothing reprograms it.

MEASURED. 0092 logged gmu->status alongside the RSC's real registers at both ends
of the sleep/wake handshake. Across 925 handshakes on a healthy boot,
RSC_STATUS0_DRV0 only ever reads:

	01000000   awake
	01010006   asleep

and it already reads 01000000 at the very first cold boot, because rpmh_init ran
at probe. The first handshake after a resume:

	21:44:00  stop:afterpoll  status=0x8  STATUS0_DRV0=01010006   last pre-hibernate
	          ...hibernate + resume...
	21:45:13  start:entry     status=0x8  STATUS0_DRV0=00000000   <-- neither state
	21:45:13  start:SKIPPED (PDC_SLEEP clear -- no wake issued)

Zero is the post-reset value. With the sequencer unprogrammed the sleep
handshake cannot complete -- "Unable to power off the GPU RSC" -- and everything
after that follows from a GPU left in a bad power state: GMU OOB GPU_SET
timeouts, "timeout waiting for GPU to idle", CP ucode error interrupt, hangcheck
recover, session gone.

WHAT THIS ALSO DISPROVES. The working hypothesis going in was that 0068 was
over-correcting: it clears GMU_STATUS_PDC_SLEEP on a cold boot, so
a6xx_rpmh_start() skips the wake, and if the RSC had really been left asleep
then a6xx_rpmh_stop() would be sleeping an already-sleeping RSC. The log kills
that: the cold boot reports "clearing GMU_STATUS_PDC_SLEEP (was clear)" -- the
bit was not set, 0068 behaves exactly as intended, and the RSC is not asleep. It
is unprogrammed. 0068 is correct and stays.

THE FIX. Detect the unprogrammed sequencer from HARDWARE and reprogram it. The
condition is a zero RSC_STATUS0_DRV0, read straight from the block -- deliberately
not a software flag, because every software flag on this path comes back inside
the hibernation image and matches by construction. That is precisely what hid
the eDP pixel clock (0090), the DPU core clock OPP (0086) and PDC_SLEEP (0068);
a hardware zero cannot be faked by restored state.

a6xx_gmu_rpmh_init() was probe-only and not safe to call twice: it does two
devm_platform_ioremap_resource_byname() calls, which would leak a mapping per
resume since devm frees at driver detach. The mappings are now stashed in struct
a6xx_gmu and reused. (On a7xx, which this board is, pdc_in_aop is true so the
seq mapping is never taken and only the RSCC writes run -- those already use
gmu->rscc, which has always been stored.)

UNVERIFIED ON HARDWARE at the time of writing: applies clean, compiles clean.
Check a resumed boot for

	RSC sequencer unprogrammed (STATUS0_DRV0=0) -- reprogramming after restore

followed by handshakes that read 01000000/01010006 like a healthy boot, and no
"Unable to power off the GPU RSC". If the line never appears, the premise is
wrong and this should be dropped.

VERIFIED ON HARDWARE 2026-09-12: the line fires on every cross-boot restore,
"Unable to power off the GPU RSC" is gone, and with 0094 the desktop survives a
resume. Regenerated 2026-09-12 against a tree without 0092, which has since been
shelved; the code is unchanged except that the new block now sits above the
0068 comment it had been inserted into the middle of.
---
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/adreno/a6xx_gmu.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/adreno/a6xx_gmu.c
@@ -691,9 +691,18 @@
 	if (adreno_is_a8xx(adreno_gpu))
 		return;
 
-	pdcptr = devm_platform_ioremap_resource_byname(pdev, "gmu_pdc");
-	if (IS_ERR(pdcptr))
-		return;
+	/*
+	 * Re-run after a hibernate restore (see a6xx_gmu_resume()), so map only
+	 * once -- devm frees at driver detach, not per call.
+	 */
+	if (gmu->pdcptr) {
+		pdcptr = gmu->pdcptr;
+	} else {
+		pdcptr = devm_platform_ioremap_resource_byname(pdev, "gmu_pdc");
+		if (IS_ERR(pdcptr))
+			return;
+		gmu->pdcptr = pdcptr;
+	}
 
 	if (adreno_is_a650_family(adreno_gpu) ||
 	    adreno_is_a7xx(adreno_gpu))
@@ -706,9 +715,13 @@
 		pdc_address_offset = 0x30080;
 
 	if (!pdc_in_aop) {
-		seqptr = devm_platform_ioremap_resource_byname(pdev, "gmu_pdc_seq");
+		if (gmu->seqptr)
+			seqptr = gmu->seqptr;
+		else
+			seqptr = devm_platform_ioremap_resource_byname(pdev, "gmu_pdc_seq");
 		if (IS_ERR(seqptr))
 			return;
+		gmu->seqptr = seqptr;
 	}
 
 	/* Disable SDE clock gating */
@@ -1371,6 +1384,34 @@
 	}
 
 	/*
+	 * The RSC/PDC sequencer is programmed exactly once, by
+	 * a6xx_gmu_rpmh_init() at probe. After a cross-boot hibernate restore
+	 * the block has been reset and nothing reprograms it.
+	 *
+	 * Measured: across 925 handshakes on a healthy boot,
+	 * RSC_STATUS0_DRV0 only ever reads 01000000 (awake) or 01010006
+	 * (asleep) -- it already reads 01000000 at the first cold boot, because
+	 * rpmh_init ran at probe. The first handshake after a resume reads
+	 * 00000000, which is neither: the post-reset value.
+	 *
+	 * With the sequencer unprogrammed the sleep handshake cannot complete
+	 * ("Unable to power off the GPU RSC"), and everything downstream --
+	 * GMU OOB GPU_SET timeouts, CP ucode error, hangcheck -- follows from a
+	 * GPU left in a bad power state.
+	 *
+	 * Detect it from HARDWARE, not from a flag. Every software flag on this
+	 * path comes back inside the hibernation image and matches by
+	 * construction; that is what hid the pixel clock (0090), the core clock
+	 * OPP (0086) and GMU_STATUS_PDC_SLEEP (0068). A zero here cannot be
+	 * faked by restored state.
+	 */
+	if (!gmu_read_rscc(gmu, REG_A6XX_GPU_RSCC_RSC_STATUS0_DRV0)) {
+		dev_info(gmu->dev,
+			 "RSC sequencer unprogrammed (STATUS0_DRV0=0) -- reprogramming after restore\n");
+		a6xx_gmu_rpmh_init(gmu);
+	}
+
+	/*
 	 * GMU_STATUS_PDC_SLEEP is software state: a6xx_rpmh_stop() sets it when
 	 * this kernel put the RSC to sleep, and a6xx_rpmh_start() then asks the
 	 * RSC to wake back up, polling RSCC_CONTROL_ACK.
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/adreno/a6xx_gmu.h
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/adreno/a6xx_gmu.h
@@ -72,6 +72,13 @@
 	u32 mmio_offset;
 	void __iomem *rscc;
 
+	/*
+	 * Kept so a6xx_gmu_rpmh_init() can be re-run after a hibernate
+	 * restore without leaking a devm mapping per resume.
+	 */
+	void __iomem *pdcptr;
+	void __iomem *seqptr;
+
 	int hfi_irq;
 	int gmu_irq;
 
-- 
